BUILDING
========

Odin is built on GNU/Linux with GCC (C and C++20), GNU make and pkg-config.
It needs the development files of SDL 2, GLEW and Jansson, and a graphics
driver that offers OpenGL 4.3.

 $ cd path/to/odin
 $ make

This makes bin/odin. An object is built again when a header it includes
changes. "make install" copies the binary to ~/.local/bin and the list of the
games, vk-games.json, to ~/.local/share/odin.


RUNNING
=======

 $ bin/odin -R <ROM directory> -r <game>

The games are those of vk-games.json: airtrix, braveff, pharrier, podrace,
sgnascar and von4. The file is looked for in the current directory, then in
$HOME, then in ~/.local/share/odin. The ROM directory holds one directory
for each game, of the name of the game, with the files vk-games.json lists
for it.

Options:

 -n <frame>		stops after that frame
 -s <frame>[,...]	saves <game>-<frame>.vkstate after these frames
 -l <file>		loads a state at startup
 -u			lifts the limit of 60 frames per second
 -v			more messages

Keys: Escape quits, Space pauses, F4 saves <game>.vkstate and F1 loads it.
F5 and F6 are the Test and Service switches of the main board, F2 the Test
of the I/O board, 9 and 0 its Service switches, 5 and 6 the coins. Player 1
has 1 for Start, the arrows, and Left Ctrl, Left Alt, Left Shift, Z, X, C;
player 2 has 2 for Start, R F D G, and A, S, Q, W, E, T.

The settings of the BIOS live in the EEPROM of the I/O board, which is
blank at every start unless ODIN_MIE_EEPROM=<file> names a file to load it
from and to write it back to on exit.

The comments at the top of the source files tell the other variables of the
environment; they are for debugging.
